  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜たら conditional",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Take the past tense (た-form) of a verb and add ら (ra). This creates a conditional meaning 'if' or 'when'. It's the most versatile conditional in Japanese.\n\nExample from this song: はばたいたら (habataitara) = 'if I spread my wings' — from はばたく (habataku, 'to flap wings') → はばたいた (habataita, past) + ら (ra)\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べる (taberu, 'to eat') → 食べたら (tabetara, 'if/when I eat')\n• 行く (iku, 'to go') → 行ったら (ittara, 'if/when I go')\n• 安い (yasui, 'cheap') → 安かったら (yasukattara, 'if it's cheap')\n\nNote: たら is the most conversational conditional. It works for hypothetical, real, and one-time conditions. It also implies a time sequence — 'when X happens, then Y'. Compare: ば (more hypothetical), と (natural/automatic result), なら (topic-based)."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "はばたく (habataku, 'to flap wings') → はばたいた (habataita, past) → はばたいたら (habataitara, 'if I spread my wings')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜のは...だ (emphasis/focus)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Wrap a clause with のは...だ to emphasize or focus on a specific element. のは nominalizes the clause ('the one who...', 'the thing that...'), and だ concludes. This is like English cleft sentences: 'It is X that...'.\n\nExample from this song: The lyrics use this pattern to focus attention on what truly matters in the speaker's journey.\n\nMore examples:\n• 教えてくれたのは先生だ (oshiete kureta no wa sensei da, 'it was the teacher who taught me')\n• 一番大切なのは友達だ (ichiban taisetsu na no wa tomodachi da, 'what's most important is friends')\n• 泣いたのは嬉しかったからだ (naita no wa ureshikatta kara da, 'the reason I cried is because I was happy')\n\nNote: This is a focus/emphasis construction, not just a statement. It draws attention to the specific element between のは and だ."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "[clause] + のは (no wa, nominalizer + topic) ... だ (da, 'it is') = emphasis pattern"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ず literary negative",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N2",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Replace the negative ない (nai) ending with ず (zu). This is the classical negative conjunctive form, meaning 'without doing' or 'not doing'. It's equivalent to modern ないで (naide).\n\nExample from this song: 覚えられず (oboerarazu) = 'unable to remember' — from 覚えられる (oboerareru, 'can remember') → 覚えられない (oboerarenai) → 覚えられず (oboerarazu, literary negative)\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べる (taberu, 'to eat') → 食べず (tabezu, 'without eating')\n• 知る (shiru, 'to know') → 知らず (shirazu, 'without knowing')\n• 言う (iu, 'to say') → 言わず (iwazu, 'without saying')\n\nNote: ず is the conjunctive form (connects to the next clause). ぬ is the attributive form (modifies nouns). Both are classical negatives. する becomes せず (sezu). You'll encounter ず frequently in anime lyrics and proverbs."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "覚えられる (oboerareru, 'can remember') → 覚えられない (oboerarenai, modern negative) → 覚えられず (oboerarazu, 'unable to remember')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜はじめる (begin to)",
